+<section id="manifest-validity">
+<h2>Manifest Validity</h2>
+<p>A Trust Manifest exists to carry verifiable trust evidence; an empty one
+adds nothing and misleads consumers into believing trust metadata is
+present. Beyond the required <code>identity</code>, a Trust Manifest MUST therefore
+contain at least one <em>substantive</em> trust member:</p>
+<ul>
+<li>a <code>signature</code> (with its required <code>subject</code> and <code>issuedAt</code>),</li>
+<li>a non-empty <code>attestations</code> array,</li>
+<li>a non-empty <code>provenance</code> array, or</li>
+<li>a <code>trustSchema</code>.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>The members <code>identity</code> and <code>identityType</code> (which restate or describe the
+entry identifier) and the informational members <code>privacyPolicyUrl</code>,
+<code>termsOfServiceUrl</code>, and <code>metadata</code> do NOT satisfy this requirement.
+<code>subject</code>, <code>issuedAt</code>, and <code>expiresAt</code> are not substantive on their own:
+an unsigned <code>subject</code> digest is attacker-settable and unverifiable, so
+they count only as part of a <code>signature</code>.</p>
+<p>A Trust Manifest that would carry only non-substantive members MUST be
+omitted entirely rather than included empty — the <code>trustManifest</code> member
+is itself OPTIONAL, so no information is lost. Consumers SHOULD treat a
+Trust Manifest that violates this rule as if no Trust Manifest were
+present.</p>
 </section>
